WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.399 --> 00:00:04.359
Film Courage: Why is screenwriting hard?

00:00:04.359 --> 00:00:10.139
Markus Redmond, Actor/Writer/Director/Producer:
It’s not.

00:00:10.139 --> 00:00:17.070
Film Courage: Should we ask Siri?

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:20.539
Markus: It’s not.

00:00:20.539 --> 00:00:25.839
Anything is hard that you are forcing.

00:00:25.839 --> 00:00:31.279
If you’re forcing it, it’s hard.

00:00:31.279 --> 00:00:34.350
Film Courage: Then maybe it’s the career
aspect that’s hard?

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:37.170
So the screenwriting process is easy?

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:39.400
Markus: Oh yeah.

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:46.260
The navigating of a career is always most
challenging then doing the actual thing.

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:55.050
Like there are tons of incredibly talented
actors that are not stars for one reason or

00:00:55.050 --> 00:01:00.430
another that has nothing to do with how talented
they are as actors.

00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:06.450
It’s the same thing with writers, directors,
musicians, songwriters.

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:11.960
A career trajectory is a completely different
skillset.

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:21.330
That’s why it’s important to either have
someone who can guide you through it or figure

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:30.060
out how to market stuff so that to appeals
to the people you are trying to get it to.

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:35.770
But yes, I said earlier, it’s way easy to
write the script than pitch it.

00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:40.330
They will ask you for like a logline.

00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:42.240
That’s a few words.

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:46.020
It takes me forever to figure it out.

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:49.890
But you want a 100 pages of a script?

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:50.890
Done!

00:01:50.890 --> 00:01:51.890
Easy!

00:01:51.890 --> 00:01:52.890
Gotcha!

00:01:52.890 --> 00:01:56.159
Now you want me to take those 100 pages and
put them in a sentence?

00:01:56.159 --> 00:01:57.159
Why?

00:01:57.159 --> 00:02:00.940
I’ve got a 100 pages you know?

00:02:00.940 --> 00:02:07.280
So they are just different skillsets but I
don’t necessarily think it’s hard.

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:10.179
It can be challenging, it can be difficult.

00:02:10.179 --> 00:02:12.010
There could be research involved.

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:18.769
It’s not necessarily easy but I wouldn’t
categorize it as hard unless you are forcing

00:02:18.769 --> 00:02:26.099
it or there are forces impeding on the creative
flow necessary.

00:02:26.099 --> 00:02:33.689
Film Courage: So were there times where you
had some who wanted to (quote) “guide your

00:02:33.689 --> 00:02:37.860
career” and sometimes it’s better to go
it alone?

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:39.689
Markus: Yes.

00:02:39.689 --> 00:02:45.340
Film Courage: Someone that saw things in you
and thought “This will be great.

00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:49.920
Let’s work together on the trajectory of
your career.”

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:56.060
And you could see early on that it would be
better to be on your own.

00:02:56.060 --> 00:03:00.370
Markus: Yes…it’s like the manager who
told me I should change the script and make

00:03:00.370 --> 00:03:04.329
it a dude (it was written about a woman).

00:03:04.329 --> 00:03:07.969
It’s like he’s not wrong.

00:03:07.969 --> 00:03:09.769
He’s not right.

00:03:09.769 --> 00:03:12.159
It’s just a difference.

00:03:12.159 --> 00:03:17.060
People get caught up in right and wrong, blame,
this and that.

00:03:17.060 --> 00:03:22.480
It’s like there is stuff that is and there
is stuff that isn’t.

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:31.909
Like whatever perception you put on it is
your perception.

00:03:31.909 --> 00:03:38.930
I get way more accomplished with that mindset
then trying to figure out right and wrong,

00:03:38.930 --> 00:03:40.909
this and that.

00:03:40.909 --> 00:03:42.769
I like this.

00:03:42.769 --> 00:03:44.499
I think this works.

00:03:44.499 --> 00:03:52.379
I think this proceeds in a manner that would
be entertaining to millions of people.

00:03:52.379 --> 00:03:54.189
What do you think?

00:03:54.189 --> 00:03:58.639
I think it should be like this, this, this,
and this.

00:03:58.639 --> 00:03:59.639
Okay?

00:03:59.639 --> 00:04:00.639
What do you think?

00:04:00.639 --> 00:04:01.999
“I kinda dig it.

00:04:01.999 --> 00:04:03.999
I think you can make some tweaks and make
it even cooler.”

00:04:03.999 --> 00:04:04.999
“What are your tweaks?”

00:04:04.999 --> 00:04:06.269
“Those sounds really cool.”

00:04:06.269 --> 00:04:07.269
“Okay!”

00:04:07.269 --> 00:04:08.610
“We should work together.”

00:04:08.610 --> 00:04:13.129
That’s all I think it comes down to.

00:04:13.129 --> 00:04:16.510
Now if it’s not a fit, it’s not a fit.

00:04:16.510 --> 00:04:17.510
It’s like anything else.

00:04:17.510 --> 00:04:22.810
It’s like friends, it’s like dating, it’s
like marriage.

00:04:22.810 --> 00:04:30.180
If you’re constantly doing this, maybe it’s
not a fit.

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:33.370
Maybe…it’s not a fit.

00:04:33.370 --> 00:04:43.310
Maybe there is a better way of getting your
work seen…heard…done.

00:04:43.310 --> 00:04:47.240
Tyler Perry was homeless trying to figure
it out.

00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:49.180
Couldn’t get anything going.

00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:52.430
He started turning his scripts into plays.

00:04:52.430 --> 00:04:54.319
Now he is sitting on top of an empire.

00:04:54.319 --> 00:04:59.319
He’s worth hundreds of millions of dollars
doing whatever he wants to do.

00:04:59.319 --> 00:05:05.580
I’m sure somebody told him “Well this
is dumb!

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:09.819
Tyler Perry you shouldn’t do that.”

00:05:09.819 --> 00:05:10.819
Maybe they were right?

00:05:10.819 --> 00:05:12.790
Maybe it could happen quicker?

00:05:12.790 --> 00:05:16.340
Maybe they were wrong and it happened the
way it was supposed to?

00:05:16.340 --> 00:05:21.530
So I just think it’s a matter of perspective.

00:05:21.530 --> 00:05:29.870
I just think the most important thing is for
you as a writer to know who you are as a writer

00:05:29.870 --> 00:05:31.080
to know who you are as a writer.

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:36.810
Like you have to know who you are and what
you do because they don’t.

00:05:36.810 --> 00:05:40.449
You know what I mean?

00:05:40.449 --> 00:05:44.120
They may have ideas about what the think it
could be.

00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:47.729
And if that links up, awesome.

00:05:47.729 --> 00:05:55.270
But you’ve got to know where you want to
go, what you want to do, how you want to do

00:05:55.270 --> 00:05:59.380
it, the kind of thing you dig, the kind of
thing you don’t, what you are good at, what

00:05:59.380 --> 00:06:01.599
you are not good at.

00:06:01.599 --> 00:06:07.830
Because otherwise you get stuck in this battle
and you’ve got an agent or a manager or

00:06:07.830 --> 00:06:12.130
an executive or a producer trying to get you
to do something that is not what you want

00:06:12.130 --> 00:06:14.460
to do and it’s not good.

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:21.099
Film Courage: So not just the writer’s voice
but what they want to do with their career,

00:06:21.099 --> 00:06:23.849
what projects they know they want to do?

00:06:23.849 --> 00:06:24.849
Markus: Yes.

00:06:24.849 --> 00:06:32.160
I think a writer should understand their identity
as a writer.

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:36.470
It doesn’t mean you can’t branch out and
do other things you know what I mean?

00:06:36.470 --> 00:06:41.389
It doesn’t mean you can’t branch out and
attack different genres and stuff.

00:06:41.389 --> 00:06:50.520
But I think you should have a very clear understanding
of who you are as a writer, just as you should

00:06:50.520 --> 00:06:53.660
have a very clear understanding of who you
are as a person.

00:06:53.660 --> 00:07:01.479
You know, people are different and they have
different attributes and different value systems

00:07:01.479 --> 00:07:04.539
and that’s who they are.

00:07:04.539 --> 00:07:08.419
For better or for worse, that is who they
are but they know who they are when they know

00:07:08.419 --> 00:07:09.419
who they are.

00:07:09.419 --> 00:07:11.430
I think a writer is the same way.

00:07:11.430 --> 00:07:13.590
This is what I do.

00:07:13.590 --> 00:07:14.699
You know what I mean?

00:07:14.699 --> 00:07:20.210
You watch the Shonda Rhimes show [Grey's Anatomy],
you know what you are getting.

00:07:20.210 --> 00:07:25.180
And now even things that she doesn’t create
but things she executive produced, still feels

00:07:25.180 --> 00:07:30.900
like the things that she wrote because she
knows what she does as a writer.

00:07:30.900 --> 00:07:33.800
This is what I do, this is it.

00:07:33.800 --> 00:07:40.669
Ryan Murphy who runs the gamut from American
Horror Story to Glee but there is still a

00:07:40.669 --> 00:07:44.040
thread in there that lets you know that’s
Ryan Murphy.

00:07:44.040 --> 00:07:45.960
That’s what he does.

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:51.280
The late Steven Bochco…that’s what he
did.

00:07:51.280 --> 00:07:52.560
You know what I mean?

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:56.129
There is an identity, there is a through line
that comes through.

00:07:56.129 --> 00:07:59.190
Film Courage: Stephen J. Cannell same thing.

00:07:59.190 --> 00:08:03.810
Markus: All of the great show runners people
know.

00:08:03.810 --> 00:08:11.379
Maybe they don’t know the title "show runner”
but these impresarios.

00:08:11.379 --> 00:08:12.840
And in film, too.

00:08:12.840 --> 00:08:22.169
Martin Scorsese who ran the gamut from GOODFELLAS
to THE AGE OF INNOCENCE.

00:08:22.169 --> 00:08:29.880
Martin Scorsese, he knows who he is.

00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:30.709
I think a writer should too.

